{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>No one shall take a mill or an upper stone in pledge.</b> The reason for 'shall not take in pledge' (לא יחבול) is that they are instruments of food and the sustenance of life. And the Torah mentioned these, and the same law applies to all other instruments with which food for life is made. And the meaning of לא יחבול is that he shall not enter the borrower's house to take a pledge — for even with instruments that are not used for making food, the creditor is not permitted, since Scripture said, ״לא תבא אל ביתו לעבוט עבוטו״ (you shall not enter his house to take his pledge), for it is impossible for the lender in any way to enter the borrower's house at all to take a pledge — and not even a court messenger (שליח ב\"ד), as it says, ״בחוץ תעמוד והאיש״ (you shall stand outside, and the man…). If so, why was it necessary to say לא יחבול, thereby transgressing two prohibitions (לאוין)?"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "Read רחים ורכב as the two millstones per the verse; 'the borrower' renders לוה and 'the lender/creditor' renders מלוה; ב\"ד expanded as 'court' per standard abbreviation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:48.597154156Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}